docs\journeys\scratch\mono-drivers-audit.md:11:3. **Pause/timeScale sensitivity exists, but I did not find a hard failure.** `RuntimeStatsOverlay` is already pause-safe because it uses `Time.unscaledDeltaTime` (`WorldSphereMod/Code/Worldspace/RuntimeStatsOverlay.cs:83-118`). The other time-driven drivers use scaled time (`Time.deltaTime`, `Time.time`, or `WaitForSeconds`), so pausing will freeze wind, water waves, the fallback day clock, profiler windows, and the auto-test coroutine rather than crash them (`WorldSphereMod/Code/Foliage/WindSwayDriver.cs:14-25`, `WorldSphereMod/Code/Water/WaterSurface.cs:130-138`, `WorldSphereMod/Code/Lighting/TimeOfDay.cs:35-45`, `WorldSphereMod/Code/Perf/ProfilerFrameDriver.cs:14-14`, `WorldSphereMod/Code/AutoTest.cs:26-73`). If the intent is “keep running while paused,” these are the places to switch to unscaled/realtime APIs.
docs\journeys\scratch\mono-drivers-audit.md:13:4. **Cleanup coverage is mostly explicit; only one driver uses `OnDisable`.** `DebugSpawnBuildingsDriver` unsubscribes from `MapBox.on_world_loaded` in `OnDisable()` (`WorldSphereMod/Code/ProcGen/DebugSpawnBuildingsDriver.cs:29-37`). Other teardown paths are `OnDestroy()` (`WorldSphereMod/Code/Lighting/TimeOfDay.cs:33-33`, `WorldSphereMod/Code/Lighting/ProceduralSky.cs:33-37`, `WorldSphereMod/Code/Worldspace/RuntimeStatsOverlay.cs:44-50`, `WorldSphereMod/Code/Worldspace/WorldUIRenderer.cs:74-78`) or explicit world-unload hooks (`WorldSphereMod/Code/Voxel/WorldUnloadPatch.cs:12-31`, `WorldSphereMod/Code/Worldspace/WorldUIRenderer.cs:39-63`). I did not see an unhandled event subscription or resource leak in the driver set I checked.
